we are continuing our special eye on  earth  our planet in peril series on this earth  day april 22nd  much of the electricity that powers the  u.s still comes from burning coal  generating that power accounts for 25  percent of our greenhouse gas emissions  that are warming the planet the biden  administration wants to bring that  number to net  zero by 2035 by shifting to more  renewable energy  but a recent cbs news poll found that 46  percent of americans think well that  goal is  unrealistic our senior environmental  correspondent that's been tracy  joins us from sparrows point maryland to  show us  one source of renewable energy that the  administration is really counting on  ben good morning to you gail good  morning so we're talking about offshore  wind and that is exactly what it sounds  like you build  giant wind turbines offshore way out  there in the ocean now  this is part of baltimore harbor and the  hope is that this is going to become  part of a hub that is going to support a  massive expansion  of the offshore wind industry in the u.s  which right now  is pretty darn small  the blades on these 300 foot tall wind  turbines gently bow in the breeze  as if part of some elaborate industrial  ballet  there are just seven operating offshore  wind turbines in u.s  waters these are five of them basically  this is offshore wind in this country  right now  yeah you could say that david hardy is  the north american ceo  of orsted a wind powered giant based in  denmark  he took us out on the bright blue waters  off the coast of rhode island  to see the first and still largest  offshore wind farm  in the united states it is cool to see  it up close  hardy says the u.s is way behind europe  where large offshore wind farms have  been used for decades  europe's offshore wind farms produce  nearly 25 000 megawatts of power  china's about 10 000 megawatts here in  the u.s  we produce 42. 42  42 so not 4 200 not 42 000  42 flats so we've got it we've got a  ways to go to catch up  the biden administration wants offshore  wind in u.s waters to produce  30 thousand megawatts of power by 2030  enough to supply more than 10 million  homes so how many homes in the u.s can  be powered by offshore wind right now  well this wind farm powers about 17  000 homes here in rhode island and all  of them  are here on block island which was  powered entirely by  diesel fuel until 2016 when those big  white blades started spinning  off its shores used to burn a million  gallons a year  sven resume lives on the island and is  one of the very few americans whose  power comes from offshore wind  doesn't get much better we're capturing  wind that we have here  all the time the biden administration  plans to speed up permitting for new  projects in federal waters  mostly along the eastern seaboard an  ideal setting thanks to a combination of  strong steady wind shallow water which  makes installing the towers easier  and dense population centers that need  power this is one of the best areas in  the world some have to update the saudi  arabia of offshore wind  the saudi arabia of offshore wind i  hadn't heard that one  lars peterson is ceo of vineyard wind  which plans to install  62 of these giant ge wind turbines by  2023  about 15 miles off martha's vineyard the  wind farm would provide  clean electricity to more than 400 000  homes and businesses  in massachusetts the blades are more  than 300 feet long so  longer than a football field every two  spins of the blades you can power home  for one day  so it's really really impressive  hardware but not  everyone is impressed some coastal  homeowners have complained the turbines  will ruin their views  and fishermen are concerned about access  to fishing grounds  and navigating around the giant towers  but the white house is moving ahead  claiming the offshore wind industry will  employ more than 44 thousand americans  by 2030. this has already transformed  our business  tierra strum owns a welding company in  baltimore  her company was hired to work on this  massive port facility in maryland  built to service the future offshore  wind industry what did that project mean  for your company  it allowed us to purchase this 20 000  square foot facility  and it allowed us to create eight  full-time jobs  so this was real money this was real  money and she's hoping more of it will  blow her way  now that those five turbines are about  to get a lot of new neighbors  most of these wind turbines that are  going to be built will be built so far  out you'll hardly be able to see them  from shore  and while it is still an open question  if they really will be able to create  44 000 new jobs in this industry as the  biden administration is promising  there's no doubt this is a huge  opportunity for the u.s when it comes to  renewable energy  anthony i know a lot of people think of  those as eyesores but i actually find  them really beautiful i do  too i do too and then when they show the  shot of blocking the view  thinking that what kind of view is it  blocking exactly they're like giant  works of art really  that's the way i feel and and the job  creation is also impressive at that  welding company  in maryland so coal jobs may go away to  some extent but if there's an off-road  for those people to retrain there are  other jobs that will  pop up i like what how ben said he hopes  more business blows tierra's way i  thought yeah  we're pulling for tierra in her business  our thanks to ben tracy
 

